bevy-horizon: let NAK cap a shader's registers, by its entry point's name or NAK_GPR_LIMIT

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 606441c9, after 0001-0013. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before the first
"diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

How many warps a Maxwell multiprocessor keeps in flight depends on how many registers each
thread uses. The Skate 3 world's fragment shader uses 56, which NAK_DEBUG=stats reports as room
for 36 warps; capped at 48 it reports 40, and register allocation spills what doesn't fit.
Whether the extra warps are worth the spills is a question for the console's GPU.

NAK_GPR_LIMIT=n caps every shader at n registers, a multiple of 8 from 24 to 248, and keys
NVK's shader cache with it. A shader whose entry point's name ends in "_gpr" and such a number,
such as "fragment_gpr48", is compiled under that cap, so one run of an application can compare
a shader with and without it: the entry point's name is part of the pipeline's hash, so the two
are cached apart. With both, the lower cap applies. NAK_DEBUG=stats lines end with the cap
applied ("cap=", 0 for none). With neither, nothing changes.

The cap reaches register allocation and the scheduler that runs before it with the shader model
(ShaderModelInfo), which each compile makes for itself, so shaders compiled at once on several
threads keep their own. That also keeps NAK's own code free of thread-locals. On the Switch NAK
is built for aarch64 Linux, whose thread-locals are read through TPIDR_EL0, a register libnx
never sets; they work only in programs that set it themselves, as bevy-horizon does. mesa-switch's
C code reaches its thread pointer through libnx instead (-mtp=soft, switch_cross_file.txt).

NAK's own NAK_STATS lines never reach the Switch's log, so on the Switch NVK says on stderr, for
the first 32 shaders whose entry point's name has "_gpr" in it, what NAK compiled: instructions,
registers, warps per multiprocessor, and spills and fills to local memory. That shows the cap
took effect.

